📘 Cleaning Hardware Components – Quick Summary

1. Why Clean Hardware?

  - Prevents overheating by improving airflow
  - Reduces risk of component failure
  - Enhances system efficiency and reliability
  - Extends hardware lifespan

2. Cleaning Tools

  - Tool	Purpose
  - Compressed air (cans or compressor)	Remove dust from fans, heatsinks, PSU
  - Microfiber cloth	Clean surfaces without leaving residue
  - Isopropyl alcohol (90%+)	Remove thermal paste, clean components (evaporates quickly)
  - Anti-static brush	Clean delicate components without ESD risk
  - Vacuum cleaner (ESD-safe)	Collect dust while blowing

3. Cleaning Steps

  - Exterior:

    Keyboard, mouse, case vents, display (with alcohol/screen cleaner)
  
  - Internal Components:

    - Component	Cleaning Method
    - Fans & heatsinks	Compressed air (use vacuum nearby)
    - RAM modules & slots	Anti-static brush
    - Power Supply Unit (PSU)	Compressed air (keep distance from coils!)
    - Motherboard & expansion slots	Anti-static brush or compressed air

4. Thermal Paste Replacement

  - Remove old paste with isopropyl alcohol and lint-free cloth
  - Apply pea-sized amount of new paste on CPU
  - Secure cooler evenly to spread paste
  - Monitor CPU temperatures in BIOS/UEFI

5. Other Peripherals

  - Printers: Use built-in cleaning cycles; clean paper trays/rollers
  - Speakers/headphones: Keep dust-free
  - External drives/ports: Dust is the biggest enemy

6. Maintenance Schedule

  - Task	Frequency
  - Clean external components	Weekly
  - Internal dust removal (desktop)	Every 3–6 months
  - Replace thermal paste	Every 1–2 years
  - Check/clean printers	Monthly

7. Precautions

  - Unplug PC before cleaning
  - Wear anti-static wrist strap
  - Use plastic nozzles on compressed air/vacuum
  - Keep distance from PSU coils (can hold charge!)
  - Avoid excessive pressure on components
